#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180341} :activity/id #uuid "90074413-5fe3-5632-8eb0-3e0dc053df00" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048376 :topic/id #uuid "9a5670e8-43ef-54b7-8f4b-81842fc55f1c" :topic/math-academy-id 3715} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180341 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180787 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf F$ be a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ and $C$ be a *closed* curve in the $xy$-plane. Imagine that $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water, and $C$ is a curve that allows water to flow through it (like a fishing net). \n\nThe **flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $\\boldsymbol C$** is a scalar number that tells us how much water is escaping the region bounded by $C.$ \n\nWe have three possible scenarios: \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n![](images/6f/6ff4d006d3fbf4a434f35a13c8591fc166ff6c74adf2b7969752b7541e486df2.png)\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/38/3812148e855a84e3233316acc8bbf59bd7cd802a874c9e4807c6efd7ca6754cf.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero, the flux equals zero.\n\nThe following vector field has zero flux across $C$ because the amount of water flowing in through $C$ equals the amount of water flowing out through $C.$\n\n![](images/56/56aef4f217e9981755826745a97e8389d86b49518ffd7d3ebc75e5ee2dc28f8b.png)\n\nThe following two vector fields also have a zero flux across $C$ because the water flow is tangential to $C$ at every point along $C.$\n\n![](images/63/637677324152af3d428e405c89025f35dbe5d71e2e8f0a0797ef3aca30bae7e1.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "ecd8591f-8e5b-50b2-91a1-4f15bd97b2c2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9521 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1e80fc64-e097-5e8d-a35c-056416216619" :step/math-academy-id 184955 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180342}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180342 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180788 :tutorial/content "If $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ The symbol $\\oint\\limits$ means integration over the closed curve.\n\nIt's helpful to spend some time understanding where this definition comes from, so let's derive it using the closed curve $C$ below.\n\n![](images/6d/6dfe8a071798c93f19507808596b7d32af90367b2666643d092a7c50afe5e929.png)\n\nFirst, we pick a point $P$ on $C.$ At this point, we draw the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the outward-pointing normal vector $\\mathbf n$ to $C.$\n\n![](images/49/498e1afbaac8bc8297213eae09cc8bf8742b29f45dda1dfafff868c67ebb9de9.png)\n\nWe wish to calculate the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ at $P.$ Now, $\\mathbf F$ is made up of two components:\n\n- a component parallel to $\\mathbf n,$ which contributes to the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C,$ and\n\n- a component perpendicular to $\\mathbf n,$ which does *not* contribute to the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$\n\nWe're only interested in the contribution made to the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$ This is given by the scalar projection of $\\mathbf F$ in the direction of $\\mathbf n.$\n\n![](images/ac/ac3aae49f372908f2f9aeb53e3fc98bff117846fca05a8c696009e443390811c.png)\n\nTherefore, the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ at $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{n}F & =\\frac{F\\cdot n}{||n||}=F\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression above represents the flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$ at a single point only. Therefore, to calculate the total net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C,$ we sum (integrate) this expression over the entire curve. This gives\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a328d4d8-bfe8-50eb-8bbd-281b3bb07afe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9522 :tutorial/title "Constructing an Integral Formula for the Flux"} :step/id #uuid "9dac0bb5-3a03-56c0-a614-dc9f045cb3e3" :step/math-academy-id 184956 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180343}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180343 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180789 :tutorial/content "Consider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$ below. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\n![](images/e6/e679efaa2612d45bbfe8b918de7148853b64eaf530af34a525ef1735e0946b09.png)\n\nBy drawing an outward-pointing unit normal vector, it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](images/fb/fbcd6fd0fadcc955212a145de9edb4654ddb92ddebb8ba31eb775cff676efeeb.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive, and we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s > 0.\n$$\n\nWhen measuring flux through a closed curve, the curve's orientation does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same in either case. \n\n![](images/a3/a3d398d382e6bc94ddde77e650d50a6b5ec050083f89f64104f6f2fd172a9239.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is also positive, and we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s > 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "36b855d8-9994-5c22-bbc4-e28065875f84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9523 :tutorial/title "Orientation-Independence"} :step/id #uuid "a000a04f-fdd8-5b25-9b23-e59a14a3592d" :step/math-academy-id 184957 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180344}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180344 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179644 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179482 :question/id #uuid "c427d9b4-2944-5edc-8e19-363de348d23d" :question/math-academy-id "e-15147"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7b8782-d1f4-5654-a18f-6ad35de8fb30" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050104}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420914}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Flux's Sign From a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "4f8760a0-8c8e-5ec6-9492-0ecc32789da6" :step/math-academy-id 184958 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180345}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180345 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180790 :tutorial/content "To calculate the flux of a vector field over a smooth, closed curve, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nIf $C=C_1\\cup C_2 \\cup \\cdots \\cup C_n$ is piecewise smooth, we have to calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ over each smooth face. That is,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+ \\cdots +\n\\int\\limits_{C_n} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ on $C_i.$\n\nAs an example, let's consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf i + (y-x)^3\\,\\mathbf j$ and the rectangle $C$ shown below. \n\n![](images/5c/5cbd62feddcce7a676a76117de09154da21abdd8b039c0e45a7a0e7d5fb00e9c.png)\n\n \n\nTo calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ over $C,$ we sum the fluxes of $\\mathbf F$ over each side:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate how to compute these integrals, let's construct the flux integral across the side $C_4,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nThe side $C_4$ and its outward-pointing unit normal are shown below.\n\n![](images/06/06fd5a2ecbd709e8e835f2a57ef3ada772526366d51e5e16474d9b8635500e8e.png)\n\nSince this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have \n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = -\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(xy\\,i+(y-x)^{3}\\,j)\\cdot (-j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(xy\\,i)\\cdot (-j)+((y-x)^{3}\\,j))\\cdot (-j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}0-(y-x)^{3}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}(x-y)^{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFollowing a similar procedure for the other sides, we can construct the full expression for the flux of $\\mathbf F$ over $C.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "472c362c-27a5-5041-90c3-4533c63b736d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9524 :tutorial/title "Calculating Flux"} :step/id #uuid "ab771459-c3a6-5f4b-9e75-cfa3f13b9add" :step/math-academy-id 184961 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180346}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180346 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179645 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179483 :question/id #uuid "15426c37-1508-5407-ab49-b26f8761795c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15148"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c8ca56-5ea8-547c-80ab-6b1f1347447e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420913}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression for Flux"} :step/id #uuid "2c288a88-997c-5ef1-98ff-24092e7506f9" :step/math-academy-id 184962 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180347}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180347 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180791 :tutorial/content "Calculating the flux of a vector field across a rectangle or square requires computing four flux integrals. However, we can often simplify our calculations if the vector field displays symmetry.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = -5\\,\\mathbf i$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown below. Let's calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n![](images/8e/8e1767b9bea45e742df4953f0ec50b2d43c9622994035342307dc2be6abba8de.png)\n\nIf $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/65/65a8c37c64580f7d1f41f32722f20dfee2b8fd98333920a0feae781ea8d9541e.png)\n\nSince the vector field $\\mathbf F = -5\\,\\mathbf i$ is parallel to $C_2$ and $C_4,$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0.\n$$\n\nThis is easy to verify, since, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}-5\\,i\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand similarly for the integral along $C_4.$ \n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince the vector field is uniform, the net inflow through $C_1$ will cancel with the net outflow through $C_3.$ That is\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n-\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "53e768e4-a3bb-5b89-83b9-a97e9ff4a921" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9525 :tutorial/title "Using  Symmetry to Simplify Flux Calculations"} :step/id #uuid "5c82115b-5b07-5765-b4a2-64382478cffb" :step/math-academy-id 184965 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180348}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180348 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179646 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179484 :question/id #uuid "f4a82102-6dd6-5a73-8370-9050e0f2323a" :question/math-academy-id "e-15149"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "349c29ae-5eb5-5454-a98f-c1adac3d7108" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048512}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540276} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540271}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Square Using Symmetry"} :step/id #uuid "3b16b4e1-26b3-5b3f-a7bb-3a7ea8b7f740" :step/math-academy-id 184966 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180349}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180349 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179647 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179485 :question/id #uuid "3f592794-e0e9-529c-9040-7b2806de2380" :question/math-academy-id "e-15150"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "446c5e28-00d3-5a17-ad90-0e66b5ec3a59" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421470}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Rectangle Using Two Symmetry Properties"} :step/id #uuid "ff88b373-d7aa-57f4-97d5-9266bed9219b" :step/math-academy-id 184969}] :activity/title "Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179866}]}
